      <!-- Detailed Review: Sierra Interactive -->
      <div id="sierra-interactive" style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1);border-radius:16px;padding:32px;margin-bottom:64px;">
        <h3 style="color:white;font-size:1.3rem;margin-bottom:16px;">Sierra Interactive</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Sierra Interactive has been building real estate websites and CRMs since 2007 and now powers the pipelines of thousands of agents across the US and Canada. Its core product is an all-in-one platform built around an SEO-driven IDX website: fast pages, schema-structured local content, property search with saved searches and alerts, lead capture throughout the site, and a CRM with behavioral automation on top. Published pricing starts at $299.95 a month on annual prepay for Starter (1 user), $399.95 for Essential (3 users), and $599.95 for Growth (5 users). Every plan includes one MLS feed, additional feeds are $25 a month, and Essential and Growth include a built-in dialer with 1,500 SMS segments and 1,500 voice minutes a month, rising to 15,000 and 5,000 on Growth. Practically every reviewer agrees the websites rank, which is why teams stay on it.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          Where it costs more than the headline, and this is where we have to be straight with you. Monthly billing under the same 12-month agreement is $359.95, $474.95, and $724.95 a month, plus a one-time $500 setup fee that annual prepay waives. It is a 12-month term either way, not month to month. Extra users cost $75 a month on Starter and $60 a month for users 4 to 10 on Essential, so a five-person Starter team is $599.95 a month before add-ons, which is another $3,600 a year for seats alone. The premium website designs (Pinnacle, Exec, Luxury) are $100 to $150 a month extra unless you are on Growth. Lead Engage, the AI lead nurture that actually puts Sierra in the AI conversation, is $199 a month and is not available on Starter at all. And if you want lead volume from ads, Google management is a 10 percent fee on a minimum $500 monthly ad spend, so $6,000 a year in ad spend before the management fee.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          The reviews are more mixed than the ratings suggest, and two themes repeat. The first is support. A long-time user on r/realtors wrote that since a private equity acquisition the original founder is no longer involved and that "now, support is handled in India, and direct communication with them is not an option," adding that "the daily support experience is quite poor. You receive automated emails, and then you're left hoping for a timely response or resolution to your issues." A G2 reviewer put it more simply: "There are some glitches with the automations that support has not been great in helping with so I have had to find my own work arounds." A 1-star Trustpilot reviewer said the platform "tanked" in seven months, that "customer service is non-existent," and that the system started sending a badly formatted mass email to their clients that they had never programmed.
        </p>
        <p style="color:var(--bs-text);line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:16px;">
          The second theme is ownership and cost. The same r/realtors user wrote that "Sierra functions primarily as a lead capture website rather than a true CRM or transaction management platform" and that you then need to buy additional software, which leads to duplicated data. Another long-time user flagged a contract change in the same thread: "My initial agreement indicated that I was on a month-to-month basis after the first year. Now, it seems I must transition my entire business within two weeks or face a higher fee for the annual contract, along with additional costs for various third-party add-ons." An industry analysis of all-in-one platforms is blunt about the structural point: "Who owns the website if I cancel? The answer, for virtually every all-in-one platform, is the vendor. The site goes dark or gets recycled the moment your subscription ends." On the price, even a five-star G2 reviewer conceded that "it's not the cheapest option on the market." If organic search is your engine and you have the volume to justify $300 to $600 a month indefinitely, Sierra Interactive earns its place, especially for teams. If you are buying it expecting AI answering or a website you keep, read the terms first.
        </p>
